            "parents": [
                "150b1684b4cc6b0753ccb01607f41c8fb5f4face"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/87/90887/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ender",
                "email": "andhsieh@nvidia.com",
                "username": "Ender-Hsieh"
            },
            "createdOn": 1780555009,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ender",
                "email": "andhsieh@nvidia.com",
                "username": "Ender-Hsieh"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/NvidiaGpuEnergySensor.cpp",
                    "line": 61,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Liu",
                        "email": "liuer@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "liuer-sys"
                    },
                    "message": "encode() is now in the ctor, which can't bail on failure. I think we can store the result\n(bool requestEncoded) and add if (!requestEncoded) return; at the top of update(), so a\nfailed encode skips the poll like before. Same dropped-guard pattern across the sensor\nclasses here (Temp/Energy/Voltage)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/NvidiaGpuEnergySensor.cpp",
                    "line": 61,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ender",
                        "email": "andhsieh@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "Ender-Hsieh"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/NvidiaGpuEnergySensor.cpp",
                    "line": 61,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ender",
                        "email": "andhsieh@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "Ender-Hsieh"
                    },
                    "message": "I've reverted the requestEncoded guard you suggested here, in favour of JY-Voon's approach in the sibling comments \u2014 the request is now a const member initialized in the constructor initializer list.\n\nReasoning: encode*Request() only returns non-zero when the output buffer is smaller than the packed bytes, but the buffer is a constexpr-sized std::array that matches the request size, so encode cannot fail at runtime. A size/encoder drift would be a build-time bug, not a runtime condition, so the guard was protecting a path that can't be hit in practice.\n\nIf you'd still prefer a guard, could you and JY-Voon settle on a direction here? Happy to go either way once you two align."
                }
            ],
